  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThe upper level pattern remains dominated by large scale troughing \nspanning from eastern Canada to the Great Lakes today. This is being \nreinforced by multiple embedded shortwaves, the most pertinent of \nwhich for Upper Michigan is currently noted dropping south across \nnorthern Ontario on water vapor vapor imagery. Radar reveals ongoing \nlake effect snow across the UP in north-northeasterly flow. \nTemperatures are currently running in the upper 30s near the \nlakeshore and closer to freezing for inland areas as of mid \nafternoon. This has kept snowfall limited to a trace at most near \nthe lakeshore with rain mixing in at times, while higher elevation \nareas west of Marquette have mostly reported an inch or two of \naccumulation to this point.\n\nStill expect lake effect to ramp up in intensity this evening into \nthe overnight hours as the aforementioned shortwave crosses the area \nand conditions become a bit more favorable. 850 mb temps will drop \nto around -9 or -10 C this evening, further increasing delta Ts as \nlake surface temperatures remain around 8-10 C. Model soundings show \na bit more moistening of the DGZ while inversion heights increase to \n8 or 9 kft, possibly as high as 10 kft. Latest REFS guidance depicts \naround a 40% chance for rates occasionally approaching an inch an \nhour tonight in the favored areas of Marquette and Baraga Counties. \nModels continue to struggle with snow ratios as today's observations \nrange from around 5:1 at the forecast office in Negaunee Township to \nas much as 9:1 in the higher terrain of Baraga County, still a far \ncry from the SLRs in the teens being depicted by most guidance. All \nin all, forecast amounts remain largely in line with previous \nforecasts. The bullseye will remain over the higher terrain of \nMarquette and Baraga Counties aided by upslope flow and colder \ntemperatures, with a general 2-4 inches expected through Friday \nmorning along the US-41 corridor between Negaunee and Michigamme \nTownship. Higher amounts of another 6-8 inches are still possible in \nthe rural areas north of US-41 and west of CR510, with the latest \nREFS giving this about a 60% chance of verifying. Meanwhile, areas \ncloser to the lakeshore including the City of Marquette will \nstruggle to see much accumulation given warmer temperatures near the \nlake, but it wouldn't be out of the question to pick up a slushy \ninch or so late tonight into early Friday morning along the \nlakeshore. Expect at least some snow across all areas of the UP \nthrough Friday, with locations outside of the primary north-\nnortheast wind belts picking up around an inch or maybe two in a few \nspots through Friday morning. Timing still looks good for the Winter \nWeather Advisory as snow will diminish in intensity by Friday \nafternoon. Lake effect could linger into Saturday across mainly \nMarquette and Alger Counties, but would expect accumulations to be \ncapped at an inch or less from Friday afternoon through Saturday \nmorning.\n\nMuch quieter weather is in store from Sunday through the first half \nof next week as high pressure builds in over the Great Lakes and \nmuch of the eastern CONUS. This looks to keep Upper Michigan dry \nthrough at least Wednesday, accompanied by temperatures climbing \nback into the 40s to 50s from Monday onward, well above seasonal \nnorms for mid November. Guidance does hint at another system \nbringing precipitation back to the UP towards the latter half of \nnext week, but confidence in specific details is low at this time.",
